  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nSeasonably cool temperatures and dry weather is forecast through\nmost of the long term, with global models and ensembles in\nrelatively good agreement. \n\nTuesday morning, a weak closed low (mentioned above) will be\nlocated over Wisconsin and heading east. Simultaneously, mid- \nlevel ridging over Ontario will help to slow the eastward \nprogression of this wave. At 250 mb, the core of the \nageostrophic convergence will be near central Missouri which \nmeans sinking air, no precipitation, and seasonably cool \ntemperatures are forecast. Another mid-level wave will then dive\nsouth out of Quebec constructively amplifying with the wave \nfrom the west. This low will cutoff from the main flow with a \npseudo Rex Block forming. This will allow the dry and cool \ntemperatures to persist through Friday. The latest run of the \nNBM and LREF is advertising an IQR of only a few degrees which \nmatches the high confidence of the WPC Cluster Analysis. \nCurrently, Wednesday and Thursday morning appears to be the \ncoolest with lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s. \n\nSaturday into Sunday, model guidance shows a mid-level wave\napproaching from the west but confidence does decrease this far\nin the extended. Some moderation in temperatures are forecast, \nbut even this far out the NBM and LREF only has IQRs of 5 to 10 \ndegrees.",
